INE"],"is-referenced-by-count":0},{"DOI":"10.9738\/intsurg-d-15-00184.1","title":["Postoperative Hemorrhage From Racemose Hemangioma of the Bronchial Artery After Esophageal Cancer Surgery"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,1]]},"author":[{"given":"Katsushi","family":"Takebayashi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Esophageal Surgery, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yasuhiro","family":"Tsubosa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Esophageal Surgery, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Satoru","family":"Matsuda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Esophageal Surgery, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Keisuke","family":"Kawamorita","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Esophageal Surgery, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Masahiro","family":"Niihara","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Esophageal Surgery, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Takeshi","family":"Aramaki","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Interventional Radiology, Shizuoka Cancer Center, Shizuoka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hiroshi","family":"Sato","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Division of Gastrointestinal Surgery, Saitama Medical University International Medical Center, Saitama, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Racemose hemangioma of the bronchial artery is a rare disease. The diagnosis of racemose hemangioma is difficult when affected patients are asymptomatic and causes unexplained hemoptysis. Selective bronchial artery embolization has become the first-line treatment for this rare disease. However, feasibility and safety of bronchial artery embolization was not reported in the case with permanent tracheostomy. A 44-year-old woman underwent endoscopic screening during which a circular tumor was detected in the cervical esophagus, which was confirmed as squamous cell carcinoma on biopsy. She underwent cervical esophagectomy with pharyngolaryngectomy through a cervical incision. Reconstruction was performed by free jejunal transfer and permanent tracheostomy. Six weeks after surgery, hemoptysis from permanent tracheostomy was observed. Computed tomography (CT) scan revealed no bleeding around the tracheostomy, and bronchoscopy could not identify the origin of the bleeding due to a large amount of blood clots obscuring the visual field. Bronchial arteriography showed a bent, meandering and dilated bronchial artery, extending to the right bronchus with vascular hyperplasia. We diagnosed primary racemose hemangioma of the bronchial artery, which was successfully treated by transcatheter arterial embolization of the bronchial artery. After embolization, bronchoscopy revealed no bleeding. Findings from this case suggest that bronchial angiography is essential for diagnosis of this disease, and selective bronchial artery embolization appears to be effective in treating this disease in patients with permanent tracheostomy. Accumulation of similar cases will help to elucidate the optimal diagnosis and treatment strategy for this condition.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["International Surgery"],"is-referenced-by-count":0},{"DOI":"10.9738\/intsurg-d-16-00129.1","title":["The Values of Several Inflammatory Markers, Including the Neutrophil\/Lymphocyte Ratio, in Patients With Pancreatic Cancer Treated by Curative Resection Followed by Adjuvant Chemotherapy"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,1]]},"author":[{"given":"Yusuke","family":"Nakayama","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Hepatobiliary and Pancreatic Surgery, National Cancer Center Hospital East, Kashiwa, Japan"},{"name":"Course of Advanced Clinical Research of Cancer, Juntendo University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Naoto","family":"Gotohda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Hepatobiliary and Pancreatic Surgery, National Cancer Center Hospital East, Kashiwa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shinichiro","family":"Takahashi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Hepatobiliary and Pancreatic Surgery, National Cancer Center Hospital East, Kashiwa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Masaru","family":"Konishi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Hepatobiliary and Pancreatic Surgery, National Cancer Center Hospital East, Kashiwa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ryuichi","family":"Hayashi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Course of Advanced Clinical Research of Cancer, Juntendo University Graduate School of Medicine, Tokyo, Japan"},{"name":"Department of Head and Neck Surgery, National Cancer Center Hospital East, Kashiwa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>The aim of this study was to determine the relationship between the values of several systemic inflammatory markers and the prognosis in pancreatic cancer patients treated by curative resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy. A total of 110 pancreatic cancer patients treated by curative resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy were reviewed for this study. Univariate and multivariate analyses were performed to identify the clinicopathologic factors influencing the overall survival, including the neutrophil\/lymphocyte ratio (NLR), platelet\/lymphocyte ratio (PLR), Glasgow prognostic score (GPS), and the direction of change of the NLR (increase or decrease) after 1 cycle of adjuvant chemotherapy, compared to the value recorded prior to the start of the chemotherapy. A multivariate analysis identified only the direction of change of the NLR after the first cycle of adjuvant chemotherapy as an independent risk factor for the overall survival (NLR decrease versus NLR increase, hazard ratio = 1.925; <jats:italic>P<\/jats:italic> = 0.044). The NLR, PLR and GPS were not identified as significant predictors of the overall survival. The direction of change of the NLR after the first cycle of adjuvant chemotherapy may help in predicting the effect of chemotherapy in pancreatic cancer patients treated by curative resection followed by adjuvant chemotherapy.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["International Surgery"],"is-referenced-by-count":2},{"DOI":"10.9738\/intsurg-d-15-00273.1","title":["Synchronous Double Cancer of the Common Bile Duct and the Ampulla of Vater Without Pancreaticobiliary Maljunction: A Case Report"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,1]]},"author":[{"given":"Ryota","family":"Tanaka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sadaaki","family":"Yamazoe","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ryosuke","family":"Amano","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kenjiro","family":"Kimura","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Go","family":"Ohira","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Keiichiro","family":"Hirata","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kohei","family":"Nishio","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kotaro","family":"Miura","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Masaichi","family":"Ohira","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kosei","family":"Hirakawa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Surgical Oncology (First Department of Surgery), Osaka City University Graduate School of Medicine, Osaka, Japan"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Synchronous double cancers in the biliary system are rare. Double cancer of the common bile duct (CBD) and the ampulla of Vater without pancreaticobiliary maljunction (PBM) is extremely rare. To our knowledge, only 7 cases have been reported previously. Here we report a case of synchronous double cancer of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater without PBM. A 63-year-old man was referred to our hospital with epigastric pain and jaundice. Computed tomography (CT) showed dilatation of both intrahepatic and intrapancreatic bile ducts, and slightly enhanced tumor in the middle part of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) showed a tuberous filling defect in the middle part of the CBD and an exposed tumor of the ampulla of Vater. Under a diagnosis of synchronous double cancer of the middle bile duct and the ampulla of Vater, pancreaticoduodenectomy was performed. Histopathologically, the ampulla of Vater tumor was well-differentiated tubular adenocarcinoma while the middle bile duct tumor was moderately-differentiated tubular adenocarcinoma. Normal mucosa was interposed between both tumors. These findings suggest that they were 2 primary tumors. Double cancer of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater without PBM is extremely rare. We report a case of synchronous double cancer of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater without PBM.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["International Surgery"],"is-referenced-by-count":0},{"DOI":"10.5185\/amp.2017\/309","title":["Electrochemical sensing of anti-cancer drug (Capecitabine) by GC-MWCNT-PAMAM (G3)-AuNps electrode"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,12,9]]},"author":[{"given":"Eagambaram Murugan","family":".","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chennakesavapuram R Akshata,","family":".","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Annie Stephy","family":".","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Advanced Materials Proceedings"],"is-referenced-by-count":4},{"DOI":"10.18081\/2333-5106\/017-3\/118-124","title":["Role of CD70 in pathogenesis of ovarian cancer cell metastasis"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,5,21]]},"author":[{"given":"Jack","family":"L. Pincheira","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Maria","family":"Wiseman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Abstract  American Cancer Society identifying ovarian carcinoma as the gynecologic malignancy with the highest case-to-fatality. Ovarian carcinoma metastasizes either by direct extension from the ovarian\/fallopian tumor to neighboring organs (bladder\/colon) or when cancer cells detach from the primary tumor. Exfoliated tumor cells are transported throughout the peritoneum by physiological peritoneal fluid and disseminate within the abdominal cavity. Extensive seeding of the peritoneal cavity by tumor cells is often associated with ascites, particularly in advanced, high-grade serous carcinomas. CD70 (encoded by the TNFSF7 gene) is a co-stimulatory factor present on B-cells, activated T-cells, and dendritic cells. CD70 is over expressed in tumor cells of various solid cancers including ovarian carcinoma, recently reported the role of  CD70 expression as a predictive marker of resistance to chemotherapy in ovarian cancers. We evaluated the expression of CD70 level in the pathogenesis of metastasis ovarian cancer cell. Seventy five tissue samples from metastatic ovarian carcinoma were evaluated by quantitative real-time PCR for CD70 and statistical analyses were performed using the Mann-Whitney test. Further, humanized anti-CD70 antibodies were investigated in xenograft mice models of ovarian cancer. Increasing expression of CD70 level was associated with increased risks for disease progression (HR = 1.04; 95% CI, 1.03 to 1.14) and death (HR = 1.13; 95% CI, 1.09 to 1.2). expression of CD70 was associated with a worse PFS and OS compared with non- expression of CD70 carcinomas. Furthermore, humanized anti-CD70 antibodies have shown significant antitumor activity in preclinical xenograft models of ovarian cancer cell.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["American Journal of BioMedicine"],"is-referenced-by-count":0},{"DOI":"10.4103\/0973-1482.204842","title":["Utilizing benchmarked dataset and gene regulatory network to investigate hub genes in postmenopausal osteoporosis"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"author":[{"given":"Zong-Sheng","family":"Yin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiao-Lu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yi-Ming","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhi-Dong","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shan-Song","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yi-Bin","family":"Du","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Journal of Cancer Research and Therapeutics"],"is-referenced-by-count":4},{"DOI":"10.4103\/0973-1482.204843","title":["Evolution of the treatment of primary central nervous system lymphoma in a Regional Cancer Center of South India: Impact of high-dose methotrexate on treatment outcome"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020]]},"author":[{"given":"Tamojit","family":"Chaudhuri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"AH","family":"Rudresha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"KC","family":"Lakshmaiah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Govind","family":"Babu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"autho